      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate document carefully.\n\n**Document-level info:**\n- Certificate number: R51/2006-DE1-07.05\n- Scheme: This is under OIML-CS... Actually the scheme field: A|B. R51/2006-DE1-07.05 - this is an OIML Certificate of Conformity. The scheme isn't explicitly stated. Hmm, the schema asks for scheme (A|B). R51 2006 edition predates the OIML CS scheme typically... The certificate is issued by PTB. There's no explicit mention of scheme A or B. I should omit if not present. Actually the certificate number format \"R51/2006-DE1-07.05\" is the pre-OIML-CS format. I'll omit scheme.\n- page_total: 4\n- member_state: Germany\n- date_issued: 2007-11-12\n- oiml_issuer_id: not present. Omit.\n\n**Issuing authority:**\n- name: Physikalisch-Technische Bundesanstalt\n- address_lines: [\"Bundesallee 100, 38116 Braunschweig\"] — maybe split: [\"Bundesallee 100\", \"38116 Braunschweig\"]. The markdown has \"Address: Bundesallee 100, 38116 Braunschweig\". I'll split into lines: [\"Bundesallee 100\", \"38116 Braunschweig\"].\n- person_responsible: Dr. Panagiotis Zervos\n- person_title: Direktor und Professor (from signature block: \"Dr. P. Zervos, Direktor und Professor\")\n- phone: not present\n- email: not present\n- website: not present\n\n**Applicants:**\n- name: Bizerba GmbH & Co. KG\n- address_lines: [\"Wilhelm-Kraut-Str. 65\", \"72336 Balingen\"]\n\n**Manufacturers:**\n- \"Manufacturer of the certified type is the applicant.\" So manufacturer = applicant: Bizerba GmbH & Co. KG, same address.\n\n**Certified type:**\n- category: checkweigher — the schema says category like \"Load cell\", \"Indicator\"... Here it's \"Checkweigher\" (automatic catchweighing instrument). I'll use \"Checkweigher\".\n- type_designations: CWM ... plus the weighing module types: WS10CW, WS20CW, WS20VB, WS6H, WS10H, WS10VK1, WS10VK2, WS10VK5, WS20VK, EM ...\n- description: \"Automatic electromechanical weighing instrument designed as automatic catchweighing instrument, checkweigher, weigh labeller or weigh-price labeller and equipped with external lever work (only weighing module series WS6H and WS10H) or without external lever work and with electromagnetic force compensation load cell (only weighing module series EM...) or with strain gauge load cells and performed as single or multi interval instrument or single or multiple range instrument.\"\n\n**model_family:**\nThe cert covers multiple module types: WS10CW, WS20CW (Tab 1), WS20VB (Tab 2), WS6H (Tab 3), WS10H (Tab 4), WS10VK1, WS10VK2, WS10VK5, WS20VK (Tab 5), EM ... (Tab 6).\n\nWait — let me re-check table captions vs tables. The markdown has:\n\n1. First table (unlabeled, after \"Specifications depending on the module used\"):\n   - Category XIII(1); XIIIH(x≥2)^3)\n   - vmax ≤1,47 m/s\n   - n ≤ 6000/5000^1)2)4), ≤5000^4)\n   - e ≥ 1 g\n   - Max ≤ 15 kg\n   - Min ≥ 50 g^5)\n   - Temperature range 0°C/+40°C\n   \n   This table is followed by \"**Tab. 1:** Technical data of the weighing modules of type WS10CW and WS20CW\". So actually the caption \"Tab. 1\" belongs to the table ABOVE it? Or below? In typical layout, the caption is below the table (\"Tab. 1: Technical data...\"). Let's see: the first table appears right after \"Specifications depending on the module used\", then \"Tab. 1: Technical data of the weighing modules of type WS10CW and WS20CW\", then another table, then \"Tab. 2: ... WS20VB\", then table, then \"Tab. 3: ... WS6H\" — then page break, then a table, then \"Tab. 4: ... WS10H\", then table, then \"Tab. 5: ... WS10VK1, WS10VK2, WS10VK5 and WS20VK\", then table, then \"Tab. 6: ... EM ...\"\n\nSo the pattern: caption below table. That means:\n- Tab. 1 (WS10CW and WS20CW) = the first table (unlabeled one): vmax ≤1,47 m/s, n ≤6000/5000, e≥1g, Max≤15kg, Min≥50g, 0/+40°C.\n- Tab. 2 (WS20VB) = the table with vmax dependent on load: ≤1,25 m/s (0<L≤1 kg), ≤1,15 (1<L≤6), ≤0,79 (6<L≤10), ≤0,93 (10<L≤15); n ≤ 2•3000/≤3000; e≥2g; Max≤15kg; Min≥200g.\n- Tab. 3 (WS6H) = table with vmax ≤0,67 (0<L<3kg), ≤1,00 (3kg≤L≤60kg); n ≤2•3000/≤6000^3); e≥10g; Max≤60kg; Min≥3kg.\n- Tab. 4 (WS10H) = table with vmax ≤1,47 (0<L<0,05kg), ≤0,25 (0,05<L<0,5), ≤0,92 (0,5<L<2), ≤0,67 (2<L<6), ≤0,50 (6≤L≤15); n≤6000/5000, ≤6000^4); e≥1g; Max≤15kg; Min≥50g.\n- Tab. 5 (WS10VK1, WS10VK2, WS10VK5, WS20VK) = table with vmax ≤0,83 (0g<L≤10g), ≤0,67 (10g<L≤200g), ≤0,50 (200g<L≤3750g); n≤2•3750/≤7500; e≥0,1g; Max≤3750g; Min≥10g.\n- Tab. 6 (EM ...) = hmm, but there's no table after Tab. 6 caption — page 4 ends with Tab. 6 caption then footnotes. Wait, that breaks the \"caption below\" pattern.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider. If captions were above tables:\n- Tab. 1 (WS10CW, WS20CW) = the table right after \"Specifications depending on the module used\"? No wait, that table comes before the Tab. 1 caption in the markdown. If captions are above, then Tab. 1 = the table after the Tab.1 caption, which has vmax dependent on load with ≤1,25/1,15/0,79/0,93 m/s, e≥2g, Max≤15kg, Min≥200g.\n- Then Tab. 2 (WS20VB) = table with vmax ≤0,83 m/s, n ≤2•3000/6000, e≥5g, Max≤30kg, Min≥1kg.\n- Tab. 3 (WS6H) = the table on page 4 with vmax ≤0,67 (0<L<3), ≤1,00 (3≤L≤60), n≤2•3000/6000^3, e≥10g, Max≤60kg, Min≥3kg.\n- Tab. 4 (WS10H) = table with vmax ≤1,47 (0<L<0,05) etc., e≥1g, Max≤15kg, Min≥50g.\n- Tab. 5 (VK modules) = table with e≥0,1g, Max≤3750g.\n- Tab. 6 (EM...) = no table follows → \"Further characteristics see page 2\"? No...\n\nHmm. Which is right? Let's think about physical plausibility:\n\nPattern check with caption-below interpretation:\n- Tab.1 WS10CW/WS20CW: e≥1g, Max 15kg → n=15000/1g? n ≤6000/5000. Max/Min = 15kg/50g = 300 intervals min... OK.\n- Tab.2 WS20VB: e≥2g, Max 15kg, Min 200g.\n- Tab.3 WS6H: e≥10g, Max 60kg, Min 3kg. WS6H — \"6\" maybe refers to 6kg? Hmm, e≥10g Max 60kg.\n- Tab.4 WS10H: e≥1g, Max 15kg, Min 50g. WS10H — \"10\" maybe 10kg max? Max is 15kg here though.\n- Tab.5 VK modules: e≥0,1g, Max 3750g, Min 10g.\n- Tab.6 EM...: missing table.\n\nPattern check with caption-above interpretation:\n- Tab.1 WS10CW/WS20CW: e≥2g, Max 15kg, Min 200g, belt speeds by load.\n- Tab.2 WS20VB: e≥5g, Max 30kg, Min 1kg, vmax ≤0,83.\n- Tab.3 WS6H: e≥10g, Max 60kg, Min 3kg. WS6H would have Max 60kg? Hmm.\n- Tab.4 WS10H: e≥1g, Max 15kg, Min 50g.\n- Tab.5 VK: e≥0,1g, Max 3750g.\n- Tab.6 EM...: no table → problem again.\n\nBoth interpretations leave Tab.6 without a table in this fragment. The document says \"Further characteristics see page 2\" and the doc is 4 pages; the fragment includes all 4 pages. So Tab. 6 \"EM ...\" table appears to be missing — possibly the table for EM modules is genuinely absent (maybe continued elsewhere) or the caption placement is below and Tab. 6's table just isn't in the OCR. Either way, I can't attribute a table to EM with certainty.\n\nGiven ambiguity, the safest approach: treat tables with the caption-below interpretation? Or caption-above? Let me look at the exact markdown again:\n\n```\n### Specifications depending on the module used\n\n<table>... Category XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2)^3) ... vmax ≤1,47 ... n ≤6000/5000 ... e≥1g ... Max ≤15kg ... Min ≥50g ... 0/+40°C</table>\n\n**Tab. 1:** **Technical data of the weighing modules of type WS10CW and WS20CW**\n\n<table>... vmax dependent ... e≥2g ... Max≤15kg ... Min≥200g ...</table>\n\n**Tab. 2:** **Technical data of the weighing modules of type WS20VB**\n\n<table>... vmax ≤0,83 ... e≥5g ... Max≤30kg ... Min≥1kg ...</table>\n\n**Tab. 3:** **Technical data of the weighing module of type WS6H**\n\nPage 3 of 4 pages\n---\n\nPage 4:\n<table>... vmax ≤0,67 (0<L<3kg), ≤1,00 (3≤L≤60kg) ... e≥10g ... Max≤60kg ... Min≥3kg ...</table>\n\n**Tab. 4:** **Technical data of the weighing module of type WS10H**\n\n<table>... vmax ≤1,47 (0<L<0,05kg) ... e≥1g ... Max≤15kg ... Min≥50g ...</table>\n\n**Tab. 5:** **Technical data of ... WS10VK1, WS10VK2, WS10VK5 and WS20VK**\n\n<table>... e≥0,1g ... Max≤3750g ... Min≥10g ...</table>\n\n**Tab. 6:** **Technical data of the weighing modules of type EM ...**\n\n4) For weighing instruments ...\n```\n\nWith caption-below: Tab.3 WS6H = table spanning page 3→4 (vmax ≤0,67/1,00, e≥10g, Max 60kg). Tab. 6 EM = no table (OCR missed it or it's on a page not captured — but this is \"a fragment of one certificate\", page 4 of 4 shown, so the EM table is just absent).\n\nWith caption-above: The unlabeled first table (e≥1g, Max 15kg) has no caption — could belong to nothing, and Tab. 6 has no table.\n\nHmm, \"Technical data of the weighing module of type WS6H\" appears at the bottom of page 3 (\"Page 3 of 4 pages\" comes after it). Then page 4 starts with the table (vmax ≤0,67 (0<L<3kg), ≤1,00...). This strongly suggests caption-above: the Tab. 3 caption at end of page 3 introduces the table at top of page 4. That makes WS6H the 60kg module.\n\nThen Tab. 6 caption \"EM ...\" at the bottom of page 4 with footnotes after... and no table. That's weird but possible if the table was omitted or if \"Further characteristics see page 2\"... no.\n\nActually wait. Let's reconsider: maybe caption-below and the Tab.3 caption is at bottom of page 3, referring to the table ABOVE it on page 3? No — the table above the Tab. 3 caption on page 3 is the one with vmax ≤0,83 m/s, e≥5g, Max≤30kg, Min≥1kg, which would then be Tab. 3 = WS6H with e≥5g, Max 30kg. Hmm, but then the page-4 table (e≥10g, Max 60kg) would be Tab. 4 = WS10H? But WS10H with Max 60kg? And then Tab. 5 caption refers to table above it (vmax by load ≤1,47...e≥1g, Max 15kg) = WS10VK1...? That seems wrong — VK modules have e≥0,1g, Max 3750g (the table after Tab. 5 caption), and 3750g suggests \"V K\" small parts weighing. WS10VK with Max 15kg and e≥1g... hmm.\n\nLet me use type-name hints:\n- WS6H: \"6\" → maybe 6 kg max? Or 60? Under caption-above, WS6H has Max 60kg. Under caption-below, WS6H has Max 60kg too? No wait.\n\nCaption-below mapping:\n- Table A (unlabeled, page 3, e≥1g, Max 15kg, Min 50g, vmax ≤1,47 flat) → Tab. 1 → WS10CW & WS20CW.\n- Table B (vmax by load 1,25/1,15/0,79/0,93; e≥2g; Max 15kg; Min 200g) → Tab. 2 → WS20VB.\n- Table C (vmax ≤0,83; e≥5g; Max 30kg; Min 1kg) → Tab. 3 → WS6H?? But WS6H with e≥5g Max 30kg... and \"H\" = with external lever work. Hmm.\n- Table D (page 4: vmax 0,67/1,00; e≥10g; Max 60kg; Min 3kg) → Tab. 4 → WS10H.\n- Table E (vmax 0,83/0,67/0,50; e≥0,1g... wait no.\n\nHold on, I need to recount the tables on page 3 vs page 4.\n\nPage 3 content:\n- Common specifications list.\n- \"Specifications depending on the module used\"\n- Table 1 (markdown order): Category^3), vmax ≤1,47, n ≤6000/5000^1)2)4) / ≤5000^4), e≥1g, Max≤15kg, Min≥50g^5), 0/+40°C.\n- \"Tab. 1: WS10CW and WS20CW\"\n- Table 2: Category^4), vmax by load (≤1,25 (0<L≤1kg), ≤1,15 (1<L≤6kg), ≤0,79 (6<L≤10kg), ≤0,93 (10<L≤15kg)), n ≤2•3000^1)2)4) / ≤3000^4), e≥2g, Max≤15kg, Min≥200g^5), 0/+40°C.\n- \"Tab. 2: WS20VB\"\n- Table 3: Category^4), vmax ≤0,83, n ≤2•3000^1)2)4) / ≤6000^4), e≥5g, Max≤30kg, Min≥1kg^5), 0/+40°C.\n- \"Tab. 3: WS6H\"\n- Page 3 end.\n\nPage 4 content:\n- Table 4: Category^4), vmax by load (≤0,67 (0<L<3kg), ≤1,00 (3kg≤L≤60kg)), n ≤2•3000^1)2)4) / ≤6000^3), e≥10g, Max≤60kg, Min≥3kg^5), 0/+40°C.\n- \"Tab. 4: WS10H\"\n- Table 5: Category^4), vmax by load (≤1,47 (0<L<0,05kg), ≤0,25 (0,05<L<0,5kg), ≤0,92 (0,5<L<2kg), ≤0,67 (2<L<6kg), ≤0,50 (6kg≤L≤15kg)), n ≤6000/5000^1)2)4) / ≤6000^4), e≥1g, Max≤15kg, Min≥50g^5), 0/+40°C.\n- \"Tab. 5: WS10VK1, WS10VK2, WS10VK5 and WS20VK\"\n- Table 6: Category^4), vmax by load (≤0,83 (0g<L≤10g), ≤0,67 (10g<L≤200g), ≤0,50 (200g<L≤3750g)), n ≤2•3750^1)2)4) / ≤7500^4), e≥0,1g, Max≤3750g, Min≥10g^5), 0/+40°C.\n- \"Tab. 6: EM ...\"\n\nCaption-below interpretation:\n- WS10CW & WS20CW: e≥1g, Max 15kg, Min 50g, vmax ≤1,47 flat, n ≤6000/5000.\n- WS20VB: e≥2g, Max 15kg, Min 200g, vmax by load.\n- WS6H: e≥5g, Max 30kg, Min 1kg, vmax ≤0,83.\n- WS10H: e≥10g, Max 60kg, Min 3kg, vmax 0,67/1,00.\n- WS10VK1/VK2/VK5/WS20VK: e≥1g, Max 15kg, Min 50g, vmax by load.\n- EM...: no table (missing).\n\nCaption-above interpretation:\n- (first table unattributed — would be... nothing? or maybe it's a generic \"depending on module\" intro? It has footnote 3) \"This specification applies only to weighing instruments with parameters...\" Hmm.)\n- WS10CW & WS20CW: e≥2g, Max 15kg, Min 200g, vmax by load.\n- WS20VB: e≥5g, Max 30kg, Min 1kg, vmax ≤0,83.\n- WS6H: e≥10g, Max 60kg, Min 3kg, vmax 0,67/1,00.\n- WS10H: e≥1g, Max 15kg, Min 50g, vmax by load (5 bands).\n- VK modules: e≥0,1g, Max 3750g, Min 10g, vmax by load (3 bands).\n- EM...: no table.\n\nWhich is more plausible? German DIN/PTB certificates typically place \"Tab. X:\" captions ABOVE or BELOW? In PTB certificates I've seen, table captions like \"Tab. 1: Technical data...\" usually appear ABOVE the table (common in German typography, tables have headings above). Yes! In German scientific convention, tables have captions above, figures have captions below. PTB being German, \"Tab. X:\" caption above the table is standard.\n\nAlso strong hint: \"Tab. 3: Technical data of the weighing module of type WS6H\" appears at the very bottom of page 3, immediately before the page break, and the table follows at the top of page 4. If the caption were below its table, the table would be on page 3 above it — but the table above the caption on page 3 is complete (has all rows ending with Temperature range). So caption-above makes sense: caption at bottom of page 3, table at top of page 4.\n\nSimilarly, \"Tab. 6: Technical data of the weighing modules of type EM ...\" appears near the bottom of page 4 followed by footnotes 4) and 5). If caption-above, the EM table is missing from the OCR/document. Hmm, but it's \"Page 4 of 4 pages\" — the last page. So Tab. 6's table would be... missing entirely? That seems odd for a real certificate. But this is a fragment (\"a fragment of one certificate\") — the instructions say \"Return one JSON object conforming to the layered schema for the content present in THIS fragment only.\" So the EM table may have been cut off in the fragment. I'll just not attribute any table to EM.\n\nAdditional plausibility check for caption-above:\n- WS10CW/WS20CW (CW = catchweigher, strain gauge): e≥2g, Max 15kg, Min 200g. vmax bands with loads up to 15kg: ≤1,25 (0<L≤1), ≤1,15 (1<L≤6), ≤0,79 (6<L≤10), ≤0,93 (10<L≤15). OK.\n- WS20VB: \"20\" → 20kg? Max 30kg... hmm, e≥5g, Max≤30kg. Or VB = ... whatever.\n- WS6H: H = external lever work (per page 2: \"with external lever work (only weighing module series WS6H and WS10H)\"). Max 60kg, e≥10g, Min 3kg. Plausible for a 60kg lever-work checkweigher.\n- WS10H: Max 15kg, e≥1g, Min 50g, vmax 5 bands with very low loads (0,05kg). Plausible.\n- VK modules (VK1, VK2, VK5, WS20VK): e≥0,1g, Max 3750g, Min 10g — high resolution small-part weighers. VK1/VK2/VK5 might denote max capacities 1.5kg/3kg/7.5kg? n ≤7500, e 0,1g → Max 750g for single? Anyway plausible.\n\nCaption-above gives a coherent story: first unlabeled table (e≥1g, Max 15kg, flat vmax 1,47, n ≤6000/5000) — hmm what would that be? Under caption-above it has no caption. Actually wait — maybe the first table belongs to \"Tab. 1\" too? No...\n\nHmm, alternatively maybe the first table IS Tab. 1's table but OCR ordering put caption after? Ugh.\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe captions are BELOW and the layout is: table then caption. Then Tab. 6 caption at bottom refers to... nothing above it except Table 6 (VK). No — Table 6 (VK, e≥0,1g Max 3750g) is immediately above \"Tab. 5\" caption? No wait, let me re-order.\n\nMarkdown order on page 4:\n1. Table: vmax ≤0,67 (0<L<3kg), ≤1,00 (3≤L≤60kg); n ≤2•3000 / ≤6000^3); e≥10g; Max≤60kg; Min≥3kg.\n2. \"**Tab. 4:** Technical data of the weighing module of type WS10H\"\n3. Table: vmax ≤1,47 (0<L<0,05), ≤0,25, ≤0,92, ≤0,67, ≤0,50 (6≤L≤15kg); n ≤6000/5000, ≤6000^4); e≥1g; Max≤15kg; Min≥50g.\n4. \"**Tab. 5:** Technical data of ... WS10VK1, WS10VK2, WS10VK5 and WS20VK\"\n5. Table: vmax ≤0,83 (0<L≤10g), ≤0,67, ≤0,50; n ≤2•3750, ≤7500; e≥0,1g; Max≤3750g; Min≥10g.\n6. \"**Tab. 6:** Technical data of the weighing modules of type EM ...\"\n\nCaption-below: table 1 → Tab. 4 (WS10H, 60kg); table 3 → Tab. 5 (VK modules, Max 15kg e≥1g); table 5 → Tab. 6 (EM, Max 3750g e≥0,1g). Then the first page-3 table → Tab. 1 (WS10CW/WS20CW, e≥1g Max 15kg), etc. And no unattributed table. Everything attributed! Under caption-below, every table gets a caption and Tab. 6 (EM...) gets the last table (e≥0,1g, Max 3750g).\n\nHmm! That's interesting. Caption-below attributes ALL 6 tables to 6 captions. Caption-above leaves the first table unattributed and Tab. 6 empty.\n\nBut the German convention (Tabellenüberschrift above) and the page-3/page-4 break (\"Tab. 3\" caption at page bottom, table at top of next page) argue for caption-above.\n\nHmm wait, with caption-below, what about \"Tab. 3\" being at the bottom of page 3? The table above it on page 3 is the WS20VB-adjacent table (vmax ≤0,83, e≥5g, Max 30kg). So caption-below → Tab. 3 WS6H = e≥5g, Max 30kg, vmax ≤0,83. Then Tab. 4 WS10H = 60kg module (e≥10g). And Tab. 6 EM... = the VK-position table (e≥0,1g, Max 3750g)? But wait — then the actual \"WS10VK1, WS10VK2, WS10VK5 and WS20VK\" caption (Tab. 5) gets table 3 (vmax 5 bands, e≥1g, Max 15kg). And EM... gets table 5 (e≥0,1g, Max 3750g, Min 10g). Electromagnetic force compensation load cells — high resolution, e≥0,1g, Max 3750g — YES! EMFC load cells are precisely high-resolution small-capacity! n ≤ 7500 with e = 0,1g, Max 3750g fits EMFC perfectly (EM = electromagnetic). That actually makes a LOT of sense: EM ... modules with e≥0,1g and Max 3750g, n up to 2•3750.\n\nUnder caption-above, WS10H would get the 5-band vmax table (e≥1g, Max 15kg, Min 50g, bands down to 0,05 kg) and WS6H gets the 60kg table, and EM... gets nothing. Hmm, also plausible: WS10H bands 0,05–15kg... \"10\" in WS10H → 10...15kg? And WS6H → 60kg? \"6H\" → 60? Eh.\n\nKey discriminators:\n1. Page break: \"Tab. 3: ... WS6H\" at bottom of page 3, table at top of page 4. Caption-below would mean WS6H's table is the one above the caption on page 3 (vmax ≤0,83, e≥5g, Max 30kg). Caption-above means WS6H = page-4 table (e≥10g, Max 60kg).\n2. Footnote 3): \"This specification applies only to weighing instruments with parameters being generally valid over the complete weighing range for all products and weighing in motion (dynamic weighing).\" — footnote 3) appears on \"Number of ranges/intervals: 2^2)3)\" in common specs AND on \"≤ 6000^3)\" in the 60kg table (page-4 table 1). Footnote 4) appears on Category in tables 2–6 and on n values.\n\nNote: page-4 table 1 has \"≤ 6000^3)\" — footnote 3 applies \"only to weighing instruments with parameters generally valid... for all products and dynamic weighing\". Interesting.\n\n3. The first unlabeled table has footnote 3) on Category: \"XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2)^3)\". Tables 2–6 have footnote 4) on Category. Footnote 4): \"For weighing instruments of the categories XIIIH(x ≥ 2) and Y(b) the number of verification scale intervals is limited to n_i ≤ 1000.\" Hmm.\n\nActually, maybe the first unlabeled table is part of the caption-below scheme where it IS Tab. 1. And the whole doc uses caption-below... but then Tab. 6 = EM gets table 5 (e≥0,1g, Max 3750g) which fits EMFC, and VK modules (Tab. 5) get table 3 (e≥1g, Max 15kg, 5 vmax bands). Hmm, VK = ? Maybe \"Vibrations...\" no. VK could be high-speed? The 5-band vmax table has ≤1,47 m/s for tiny loads (0<L<0,05kg) — very fast sorting of small items, Max 15kg. WS10VK — \"10\" → 10...15kg. Plausible too!\n\nAnd WS6H (Tab. 3, caption-below): e≥5g, Max 30kg, vmax ≤0,83 flat. WS6H \"6\" → 6...30kg? Hmm.\nAnd WS10H (Tab. 4, caption-below): e≥10g, Max 60kg. \"10\" → 10g scale interval! WS10H: e = 10g! And WS6H... e=5g? Hmm, no.\n\nUnder caption-above: WS6H: e≥10g Max 60kg; WS10H: e≥1g Max 15kg.\nUnder caption-below: WS6H: e≥5g Max 30kg; WS10H: e≥10g Max 60kg.\n\nThe \"WS10H = e 10g\" coincidence under caption-below is cute. And \"WS20VB\": caption-below → e≥2g (\"20\"→2?) Max 15kg; caption-above → e≥5g Max 30kg. Hmm \"WS20\" → Max 20kg? Neither is 20. VB?\n\nHonestly both are defensible. Let me think about the page-3/4 break more carefully, since that's layout evidence.\n\nPage 3 ends with:\n```\n**Tab. 3:** &nbsp;&nbsp; **Technical data of the weighing module of type WS6H**\n\nPage 3 of 4 pages\n---\n```\n\nIf captions were below tables, having a caption as the last element of a page with its table on the previous page is fine typographically (table at top/middle of page 3, caption below it, then... nothing else on page 3? Actually page 3 would then end right after the caption — fine).\n\nIf captions were above, caption orphaned at bottom of page 3 with table on page 4 — that's bad typography (widowed caption), which editors avoid but happens.\n\nHmm, actually wait. Real PTB OIML certificates — I recall R51/2006-DE1-07.05 Bizerba. I can't recall the actual document. Let me think about which is more common in these PTB OIML certs: I believe PTB certificates format tables with the \"Tab. X:\" caption ABOVE the table (like \"Tab. 1: Technical data...\" followed by the table). Actually, in many PTB OIML certificates I've seen (e.g., R60 certs), it's like:\n\n\"Tab. 1: Technical data of load cell C16iC3\n<table>...</table>\"\n\nHmm, I genuinely think German convention puts table captions above. Also the EM point: under caption-above, EM table missing; under caption-below, EM gets the high-res table.\n\nBut under caption-below, the FIRST table (unlabeled, right under \"Specifications depending on the module used\") is Tab. 1 for WS10CW and WS20CW. That works: every table attributed, Tab 1-6. Under caption-above, the first table is unattributed — it would float with no caption, which is also weird.\n\nGiven caption-below attributes everything cleanly 1:1, while caption-above leaves one table caption-less and one caption table-less, caption-below seems structurally more consistent with the OCR sequence... UNLESS the fragment is truly cut (fragment says \"a fragment of one certificate\" but includes pages 1-4 of 4, so nothing is cut — page 4 ends with footnotes and the important note, i.e., the document is complete). If the document is complete, caption-above leaves Tab. 6 (EM) without a table, which would be a real certificate defect. Therefore caption-below must be correct: captions below tables.\n\nWait, but is the document complete? The fragment includes \"Page 4 of 4 pages\" and the important note — yes, complete. So Tab. 6 EM... must have a table, and the only unclaimed table under caption-above is the first one — but that's on page 3, far from Tab. 6. So caption-above is impossible for a complete document (unless Tab. 6's table is genuinely absent, which would be a certificate defect). Caption-below gives a perfect 1:1 mapping:\n\n- Tab. 1 → WS10CW and WS20CW: first table (Category^3, vmax ≤1,47, n ≤6000/5000^1)2)4)/≤5000^4), e≥1g, Max ≤15kg, Min ≥50g^5), 0/+40°C).\n- Tab. 2 → WS20VB: table 2 (Category^4, vmax by load ≤1,25 (0<L≤1), ≤1,15 (1<L≤6), ≤0,79 (6<L≤10), ≤0,93 (10<L≤15); n ≤2•3000^1)2)4)/≤3000^4); e≥2g; Max ≤15kg; Min ≥200g^5).\n- Tab. 3 → WS6H: table 3 (Category^4, vmax ≤0,83; n ≤2•3000^1)2)4)/≤6000^4); e≥5g; Max ≤30kg; Min ≥1kg^5).\n- Tab. 4 → WS10H: table 4 (Category^4, vmax ≤0,67 (0<L<3kg), ≤1,00 (3kg≤L≤60kg); n ≤2•3000^1)2)4)/≤6000^3); e≥10g; Max ≤60kg; Min ≥3kg^5).\n- Tab. 5 → WS10VK1, WS10VK2, WS10VK5, WS20VK: table 5 (vmax ≤1,47 (0<L<0,05kg), ≤0,25 (0,05<L<0,5kg), ≤0,92 (0,5<L<2kg), ≤0,67 (2<L<6kg), ≤0,50 (6kg≤L≤15kg); n ≤6000/5000^1)2)4)/≤6000^4); e≥1g; Max ≤15kg; Min ≥50g^5).\n- Tab. 6 → EM ...: table 6 (vmax ≤0,83 (0g<L≤10g), ≤0,67 (10g<L≤200g), ≤0,50 (200g<L≤3750g); n ≤2•3750^1)2)4)/≤7500^4); e≥0,1g; Max ≤3750g; Min ≥10g^5).\n\nSanity: EM (electromagnetic force compensation) → e≥0,1g, Max 3750g, high resolution n 7500 — very sensible for EMFC. WS6H (with lever work H) → 30kg Max. WS10H → 60kg Max with lever work. Hmm \"WS10H = 60kg\"? The description says external lever work only for WS6H and WS10H — those are the H series. WS6H 30kg and WS10H 60kg, e 5g/10g. Plausible!\n\nVK modules: WS10VK1/VK2/VK5, WS20VK → e≥1g, Max 15kg, fast speeds (1,47 m/s for <50g items) — high-speed checkweighing. Plausible.\n\nWS10CW/WS20CW → e≥1g, Max 15kg, vmax 1,47 flat, n ≤6000/5000.\n\nOK I'm fairly confident in caption-below now. \n\nHmm, one more consideration: footnote 3) on the first table's Category and on WS10H's n \"≤6000^3)\". Footnote 3 says \"This specification applies only to weighing instruments with parameters being generally valid over the complete weighing range for all products and weighing in motion (dynamic weighing). For single products having especially their own Price Look Up and/or their own product-specific parameters greater tare loads are allowed if they are approved by the LMC.\" OK, that's just a footnote.\n\nNow, how to structure this data?\n\n**model_family.models**: one entry per module type:\n- WS10CW\n- WS20CW\n- WS20VB\n- WS6H\n- WS10H\n- WS10VK1\n- WS10VK2\n- WS10VK5\n- WS20VK\n- EM ... (series, no specific data)\n\nfamily_name: null (no explicit family name; \"CWM ...\" is the certified type designation... actually Type: CWM ... Hmm, \"Checkweigher Type: CWM ...\" — the type designation is \"CWM ...\" which might be a series covering modules. So family_name could be \"CWM\". The modules WS..., EM... are weighing modules of the checkweigher type CWM. I'd say family_name: \"CWM\" — it's the type under which the family is certified. Hmm, \"include ONLY if cert covers >1 model variant\" — yes it does. family_name: \"CWM\" seems reasonable since Type: CWM ... is the certified type. Or null. I'll use \"CWM\" with model entries for the modules.\n\n**type_designations**: [\"CWM\", \"WS10CW\", \"WS20CW\", \"WS20VB\", \"WS6H\", \"WS10H\", \"WS10VK1\", \"WS10VK2\", \"WS10VK5\", \"WS20VK\", \"EM ...\"]. Hmm \"EM ...\" with ellipsis denotes a series (EM plus suffix). I'll include \"EM ...\" as-is or \"EM\". I'll use \"EM ...\" to be faithful. Also WS6H and WS10H are \"weighing module series\". I'll list all.\n\n**characteristics.type_level** (common specifications):\n- mode_of_operation: \"Start-Stop operation or operation in motion\" (string value)\n- maximum_belt_speed: {min: null, max: 1.47}, unit \"m/s\" — note \"≤ 1,47 m/s\" → 1.47.\n- maximum_capacity: {min: null, max: 60}, unit \"kg\"\n- verification_scale_interval: {min: 0.1, max: null}, unit \"g\" — \"e ≥ 0.1 g\"\n- verification_scale_intervals_n: {min: null, max: 7500} — \"n ≤ 7500\"\n- power_supply_voltage: 230, unit \"V\"\n- power_supply_type: \"AC\"\n- power_supply_frequency: {min: 50, max: 60}, unit \"Hz\"\n- category: \"XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2)\" — string\n- number_of_ranges_intervals: 2, footnote_markers [\"2)\", \"3)\"] — \"2^2) 3)\". Hmm, the superscripts \"2) 3)\" are footnotes. Value 2 with footnote markers [\"2)\", \"3)\"].\n- ratio_between_verification_scale_intervals: \"e_{i+1}/e_i < 3\" with footnotes [\"1)\", \"2)\"]. The label: ratio e_{i+1}/e_i < 3. Canonical: \"ratio_between_verification_scale_intervals\". Value: hmm, it's a formula/threshold: \"e(i+1)/ei < 3\". Structured form wants value + unit. I could do value: {min: null, max: 3}? That loses the formula. Better: value as string \"< 3\" — but \"pure\" value should be numeric... The rule says formulas/symbols kept as string. I'll use \"value\": \"e(i+1)/ei < 3\"? Hmm, that mixes. Alternative: attribute \"ratio_between_verification_scale_intervals_ei1_over_ei\", value: {min: null, max: 3}, unit null, footnote_markers [\"1)\",\"2)\"]. I think that's cleaner: value {max: 3}. But the expression e_{i+1}/e_i is part of the attribute semantics. Let me name attribute \"ratio_between_verification_scale_intervals\" and value {min:null, max:3}, and maybe keep formula in attribute name? The schema wants snake_case canonical labels. I'll do attribute: \"ratio_between_verification_scale_intervals\", value {max: 3}, footnote_markers [\"1)\", \"2)\"]. Good enough.\n\nFootnote markers format: The markdown shows \"1)\", \"2)\", \"3)\" style (e.g., \"50 g^5)\"). The schema example used [\"1)\"]. I'll use [\"1)\"] etc. For \"2^2) 3)\" → footnote_markers: [\"2)\", \"3)\"].\n\n**model_level**: For each module type, the differing attributes: category (all same actually, footnote differs), maximum_belt_speed (varies, some load-dependent), n (varies), e (varies), Max (varies), Min (varies), temperature range (all same 0/+40°C → type_level? It's repeated per table but constant; could put in type_level. The common specs don't list temperature. I'll put temperature_range in model_level per model, or type_level. Since identical across all models, type_level is cleaner: temperature_range: {min: 0, max: 40}, unit \"°C\". But it only appears in module tables... The common specifications section doesn't include temperature. I'll include it as model_level? Hmm. The rule: type_level for whole-cert attributes — single value. Temperature 0/+40°C applies to all models → type_level. I'll put it in type_level.\n\nmodel_level attributes:\n1. maximum_belt_speed — varies per model, and for some models load-dependent (config-like). How to encode? This is a config_level thing: axis = load range. Let me use config_level for load-dependent belt speeds:\n\nconfig_level entries:\n- attribute: \"maximum_belt_speed\", axis: \"load\", values per model? But config_level values are {condition, value} — condition describes the dimension. But which model? Config-level entries would need model association... The schema: `config_level[]: {attribute, axis, values[]: {condition, value}}`. There's no model field in config_level values. Hmm.\n\nOptions: encode load-dependent belt speeds in matrix_tables (per-model tables), and put a simplified model_level max belt speed? Or put belt speed per model as the flat values only for models with a single flat value (WS10CW/WS20CW: ≤1,47; WS6H: ≤0,83) and for load-dependent ones use... hmm.\n\nBetter approach: use `matrix_tables[]` for each Tab. 1–6 with rows describing belt-speed-vs-load bands, and `characteristics.model_level` for scalar attributes (e, Max, Min, n) per model. And maybe config_level for belt speed with conditions like \"WS20VB: 0 < L ≤ 1 kg\". Actually I can include the model in the condition string, or add a \"model\" key — schema says values: {condition, value}. I'll use matrix_tables for the belt speed tables to preserve fidelity, plus config_level entries for load-dependent belt speeds with conditions embedding the model.\n\nHmm, let me think about what's cleanest and most faithful:\n\nmatrix_tables: one per Tab. 1–6:\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"Tab. 1: Technical data of the weighing modules of type WS10CW and WS20CW\",\n  \"columns\": [\"category\", \"max_belt_speed_condition\", \"max_belt_speed\", \"n_condition\", \"n\", ...]\n}\n```\nHmm, the tables aren't really multi-column matrices; they're attribute-value lists. The schema example for matrix_tables is for 2-D tables. These Tab tables are vertical key-value tables with some multi-row cells (belt speed vs load bands, n with two alternatives). I could represent each Tab as a matrix_table with columns [\"attribute\", \"value\"] and rows per attribute. But that's just a dict. Alternatively, for the belt-speed-vs-load bands, config_level works nicely:\n\nconfig_level:\n- attribute: \"maximum_belt_speed\", axis: \"load\", values: [\n    {\"condition\": \"WS20VB: 0 < L ≤ 1 kg\", ...}]\n\nHmm, but conditions should be the axis value only. The schema's config_level doesn't have a model field, so maybe the intended use is per-cert axes. Given the complexity, I think the best representation:\n\n- model_level for: category, max n (as two alternative interpretations... hmm n also has multi-interval/multi-range variants), e, Max, Min.\n- config_level for load-dependent belt speed: axis \"load\", with condition strings including the module: e.g. {\"condition\": \"WS20VB, 0 < L ≤ 1 kg\", \"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 1.25}, \"unit_symbol\": \"m/s\"}. And for models with a single belt speed (WS10CW/WS20CW ≤1,47; WS6H ≤0,83), either config_level with condition \"WS10CW/WS20CW: all loads\" or model_level \"maximum_belt_speed\". Mixing is awkward.\n\nAlternative: put maximum_belt_speed entirely in config_level:\nvalues:\n- {\"condition\": \"WS10CW/WS20CW\", \"value\": {max: 1.47}}\n- {\"condition\": \"WS20VB, 0 < L ≤ 1 kg\", \"value\": {max: 1.25}}\n- {\"condition\": \"WS20VB, 1 < L ≤ 6 kg\", \"value\": {max: 1.15}}\n- {\"condition\": \"WS20VB, 6 kg < L ≤ 10 kg\", \"value\": {max: 0.79}}\n- {\"condition\": \"WS20VB, 10 kg < L ≤ 15 kg\", \"value\": {max: 0.93}}\n- {\"condition\": \"WS6H\", \"value\": {max: 0.83}}\n- {\"condition\": \"WS10H, 0 < L < 3 kg\", \"value\": {max: 0.67}}\n- {\"condition\": \"WS10H, 3 kg ≤ L ≤ 60 kg\", \"value\": {max: 1.00}}\n- {\"condition\": \"WS10VK1/WS10VK2/WS10VK5/WS20VK, 0 < L < 0.05 kg\", \"value\": {max: 1.47}}\n- {\"condition\": \"WS10VK1/WS10VK2/WS10VK5/WS20VK, 0.05 < L < 0.5 kg\", \"value\": {max: 0.25}}\n- {\"condition\": \"WS10VK1/WS10VK2/WS10VK5/WS20VK, 0.5 < L < 2 kg\", \"value\": {max: 0.92}}\n- {\"condition\": \"WS10VK1/WS10VK2/WS10VK5/WS20VK, 2 < L < 6 kg\", \"value\": {max: 0.67}}\n- {\"condition\": \"WS10VK1/WS10VK2/WS10VK5/WS20VK, 6 kg ≤ L ≤ 15 kg\", \"value\": {max: 0.50}}\n- {\"condition\": \"EM ..., 0 g < L ≤ 10 g\", \"value\": {max: 0.83}}\n- {\"condition\": \"EM ..., 10 g < L ≤ 200 g\", \"value\": {max: 0.67}}\n- {\"condition\": \"EM ..., 200 g < L ≤ 3750 g\", \"value\": {max: 0.50}}\n\nWait — under caption-below, which table has the 5-band vmax (≤1,47 for 0<L<0,05 etc.)? That's Tab. 5 → WS10VK1/VK2/VK5/WS20VK. And the 3-band (≤0,83 (0g<L≤10g)...) is Tab. 6 → EM. And the 4-band (≤1,25...) is Tab. 2 → WS20VB. And 2-band (≤0,67/≤1,00) is Tab. 4 → WS10H.\n\nLet me now re-derive each table's content under caption-below carefully:\n\n**Table 1 (page 3, first) → Tab. 1 → WS10CW and WS20CW:**\n- Category: XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2) ³)\n- vmax ≤ 1,47 m/s\n- n ≤ 6000/5000 ¹)²)⁴) ; ≤ 5000 ⁴)\n- e ≥ 1 g\n- Max ≤ 15 kg\n- Min ≥ 50 g ⁵)\n- Temperature 0/+40 °C\n\n**Table 2 → Tab. 2 → WS20VB:**\n- Category: XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2) ⁴)\n- vmax ≤ 1,25 m/s (0 < L ≤ 1 kg); ≤ 1,15 (1 < L ≤ 6 kg); ≤ 0,79 (6 kg < L ≤ 10 kg); ≤ 0,93 (10 kg < L ≤ 15 kg)\n- n ≤ 2•3000 ¹)²)⁴); ≤ 3000 ⁴)\n- e ≥ 2 g\n- Max ≤ 15 kg\n- Min ≥ 200 g ⁵)\n- Temp 0/+40 °C\n\n**Table 3 → Tab. 3 → WS6H:**\n- Category XIII(1); XIIIH(x≥2) ⁴)\n- vmax ≤ 0,83 m/s\n- n ≤ 2•3000 ¹)²)⁴); ≤ 6000 ⁴)\n- e ≥ 5 g\n- Max ≤ 30 kg\n- Min ≥ 1 kg ⁵)\n- Temp 0/+40 °C\n\n**Table 4 → Tab. 4 → WS10H:**\n- Category ⁴)\n- vmax ≤ 0,67 (0 < L < 3 kg); ≤ 1,00 (3 kg ≤ L ≤ 60 kg)\n- n ≤ 2•3000 ¹)²)⁴); ≤ 6000 ³)\n- e ≥ 10 g\n- Max ≤ 60 kg\n- Min ≥ 3 kg ⁵)\n- Temp 0/+40 °C\n\n**Table 5 → Tab. 5 → WS10VK1, WS10VK2, WS10VK5, WS20VK:**\n- Category ⁴)\n- vmax ≤ 1,47 (0 < L < 0,05 kg); ≤ 0,25 (0,05 < L < 0,5 kg); ≤ 0,92 (0,5 < L < 2 kg); ≤ 0,67 (2 < L < 6 kg); ≤ 0,50 (6 kg ≤ L ≤ 15 kg)\n- n ≤ 6000/5000 ¹)²)⁴); ≤ 6000 ⁴)\n- e ≥ 1 g\n- Max ≤ 15 kg\n- Min ≥ 50 g ⁵)\n- Temp 0/+40 °C\n\n**Table 6 → Tab. 6 → EM ...:**\n- Category ⁴)\n- vmax ≤ 0,83 (0 g < L ≤ 10 g); ≤ 0,67 (10 g < L ≤ 200 g); ≤ 0,50 (200 g < L ≤ 3750 g)\n- n ≤ 2•3750 ¹)²)⁴); ≤ 7500 ⁴)\n- e ≥ 0,1 g\n- Max ≤ 3750 g\n- Min ≥ 10 g ⁵)\n- Temp 0/+40 °C\n\nHmm wait, now I doubt myself again. Under caption-above:\n- Tab. 1 WS10CW/WS20CW = table 2 (vmax 4-band ≤1,25..., e≥2g, Max 15kg, Min 200g, n 2•3000/3000)\n- Tab. 2 WS20VB = table 3 (vmax ≤0,83, e≥5g, Max 30kg, Min 1kg, n 2•3000/6000)\n- Tab. 3 WS6H = table 4 (vmax ≤0,67/≤1,00, e≥10g, Max 60kg, Min 3kg, n 2•3000/6000³))\n- Tab. 4 WS10H = table 5 (vmax 5-band ≤1,47@<0,05..., e≥1g, Max 15kg, Min 50g, n 6000/5000, 6000⁴))\n- Tab. 5 VK = table 6 (vmax 3-band, e≥0,1g, Max 3750g, Min 10g, n 2•3750/7500)\n- Tab. 6 EM = nothing.\n\nDeciding factor: completeness of document (4 pages, complete). Caption-above → Tab. 6 has no table → document defect → unlikely. Caption-below → all tables claimed. Therefore caption-below. I'm going with caption-below.\n\nAdditional support for caption-below: \"Tab. 3\" caption at bottom of page 3 — if captions go below tables, the table (table 3) is directly above it on page 3. ✓. And page 4 begins with table 4, whose caption \"Tab. 4\" follows. ✓. And the certificate begins the module section with \"Specifications depending on the module used\" followed immediately by table 1, then caption Tab. 1. ✓ consistent.\n\nGreat: caption-below confirmed by layout consistency.\n\nNow the \"WS6H = 30kg\" / \"WS10H = 60kg\" assignments... fine, H = with lever work. And EM ... = 3750g high-res EMFC. \n\nNow let me build the JSON.\n\n**model_family:**\n```json\n{\n  \"family_name\": \"CWM\",\n  \"models\": [\n    {\"model_id\": \"WS10CW\", \"label\": \"weighing module WS10CW\", \"attributes\": {}},\n    {\"model_id\": \"WS20CW\"},\n    {\"model_id\": \"WS20VB\"},\n    {\"model_id\": \"WS6H\"},\n    {\"model_id\": \"WS10H\"},\n    {\"model_id\": \"WS10VK1\"},\n    {\"model_id\": \"WS10VK2\"},\n    {\"model_id\": \"WS10VK5\"},\n    {\"model_id\": \"WS20VK\"},\n    {\"model_id\": \"EM ...\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nMaybe include \"EM ...\" as \"EM ...\" since exact designations not given (series). Fine.\n\n**characteristics.type_level:**\n```json\n{\n  \"mode_of_operation\": {\"value\": \"Start-Stop operation or operation in motion\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"maximum_belt_speed\": {\"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 1.47}, \"unit_symbol\": \"m/s\", \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"maximum_capacity\": {\"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 60}, \"unit_symbol\": \"kg\", \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"verification_scale_interval\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 0.1, \"max\": null}, \"unit_symbol\": \"g\", \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"verification_scale_intervals\": {\"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 7500}, \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"power_supply_voltage\": {\"value\": 230, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\", \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"power_supply_type\": {\"value\": \"AC\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"power_supply_frequency\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 50, \"max\": 60}, \"unit_symbol\": \"Hz\", \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"accuracy_category\": {\"value\": \"XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2)\", ...},\n  \"number_of_ranges_intervals\": {\"value\": 2, \"footnote_markers\": [\"2)\", \"3)\"]},\n  \"ratio_between_verification_scale_intervals\": {\"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 3}, \"footnote_markers\": [\"1)\", \"2)\"]},\n  \"temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 0, \"max\": 40}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}\n}\n```\n\nHmm, \"Category: XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2)\" — the label. Canonical snake_case: \"accuracy_category\" or \"category\". The certified_type.category field is \"Checkweigher\" (instrument type). The \"Category\" in tables refers to OIML accuracy class category (XIII, XIIIH). I'll use \"category\" for the attribute name to stay faithful, value \"XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2)\". Hmm, but it might be confused with instrument category. Actually R51 accuracy classes are XIII, XIIIH (and Y). I'll label it \"accuracy_category\". Hmm, schema says characteristics.type_level: <label>: {value, unit, footnote_markers}. No strict naming beyond canonical snake_case. \"accuracy_category\" is fine and clearer. Actually, to be faithful I'll use \"category\" in matrix_tables columns but \"accuracy_category\" in type_level? Inconsistent. Let me just use \"category\" everywhere for this attribute; the instrument category is captured in certified_type.category. Risk of confusion... I'll use \"accuracy_category\" for clarity in characteristics and \"category\" in matrix_tables raw rows. Hmm. Actually let me keep it simple: use \"accuracy_category\" in both. The tables list \"Category\" → accuracy_category.\n\nAlso \"for accuracy class XIII(1)\" in the recommendation section: accuracy_classes: [\"XIII(1)\", \"XIIIH\"]? The certificate page 1 says \"for accuracy class XIII(1)\". The common specs say \"XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2)\". For recomm