lines: In PTB certs for multi-interval instruments, \"n ≤ 2 • 3000\" means total across intervals (2 intervals × 3000 each). The second line \"≤ 3000\" might be for single-interval. Footnote 1) \"This applies to multiple range instruments\", 2) \"This applies to multi-interval instruments\" — these attach to \"2•\". So \"2 • 3000^(1)2)\" = the factor 2 applies for multi-range/multi-interval. The line beneath \"≤ 3000^4)\" = single range/interval limit of 3000. And in table 1: \"≤ 6000/5000^1)2)4)\" — hmm 6000/5000 with slash might mean 6000 (XIII) / 5000 (XIIIH)? And second line \"≤ 5000^4)\".\n\nGiven the deep ambiguity, matrix_tables with verbatim strings is the right call, plus a best-effort model_level with primary values. Actually — maybe I should NOT put n in model_level at all (avoid fabricating interpretation) and keep it only in matrix_tables rows as strings. But then model_level misses a key attribute... The schema example shows model_level for qmax/qt/qmin which were unambiguous. For n, ambiguity is real. I'll include model_level \"max_verification_scale_intervals_n\" using the dominant number (6000 or 7500) with footnote markers as in the first line, since \"≤ 2•3000\" numerically = 6000 max. That's defensible: the maximum is 2•3000 = 6000.\n\nSo:\n- WS10CW, WS20CW: 6000 [\"1)\",\"2)\",\"4)\"]\n- WS20VB: 6000 [\"1)\",\"2)\",\"4)\"]\n- WS6H: 6000 [\"1)\",\"2)\",\"4)\"]\n- WS10H: 6000 [\"1)\",\"2)\",\"4)\"]\n- WS10VK1, WS10VK2, WS10VK5, WS20VK: 6000 [\"1)\",\"2)\",\"4)\"]\n- EM: 7500 [\"1)\",\"2)\",\"4)\"]\n\nAnd a second attribute \"max_verification_scale_intervals_n_single_range_or_single_interval\":\n- WS10CW/WS20CW: 5000 [\"4)\"]\n- WS20VB: 3000 [\"4)\"]\n- WS6H: 6000 [\"4)\"]\n- WS10H: 6000 [\"3)\"]\n- VK*: 6000 [\"4)\"]\n- EM: 7500 [\"4)\"]\n\nWait, WS10H (table 4): \"≤ 2 • 3000^1)2)4) / ≤ 6000^3)\" — second line 6000 with footnote 3! OK.\nTable 3 (WS6H): \"≤ 2 • 3000^1)2)4) / ≤ 6000^4)\" — second line 6000 footnote 4.\nTable 1: \"≤ 6000/5000^1)2)4) 