ment, checkweigher, weigh labeller or weigh-price labeller; equipped with external lever work (only weighing module series WS6H and WS10H) or without external lever work; with electromagnetic force compensation load cell (only weighing module series EM ...) or with strain gauge load cells; performed as single or multi interval instrument or single or multiple range instrument.\" Keep close to original.\n\n**model_level summary:**\n\nverification_scale_interval (e, min, g):\n- WS10CW: 1; WS20CW: 1; WS20VB: 2; WS6H: 5; WS10H: 10; WS10VK1: 1; WS10VK2: 1; WS10VK5: 1; WS20VK: 1; EM: 0.1.\n\nmaximum_load (Max, max bound):\n- WS10CW: 15 kg; WS20CW: 15; WS20VB: 15; WS6H: 30; WS10H: 60; WS10VK1: 15; WS10VK2: 15; WS10VK5: 15; WS20VK: 15; EM: 3750 g.\n\nUnits differ for EM (g vs kg). model_level has a single unit_symbol per attribute. EM Max = 3750 g = 3.75 kg. Options: unit \"g\" for all: WS*: 15000 g... or unit \"kg\" with EM: 3.75. Rule: don't fabricate/convert? Converting units is a normal normalization but the instruction emphasizes keeping raw. Since one attribute has one unit, I'll use \"kg\" and EM value 3.75? Hmm, or \"g\" and others 15000/30000/60000. The source says \"≤ 3750 g\" and \"≤ 15 kg\". I think using kg with EM 3.75 kg is a unit conversion — slight fabrication but mathematically exact. Alternatively keep unit per attribute as \"kg\" and note EM in g within matrix_tables verbatim. I'll do model_level in kg (3.75 for EM) and matrix_tables keeps \"≤ 3750 g\" verbatim. Same for Min: EM 10 g = 0.01 kg; WS6H 1 kg; WS10H 3 kg; others 0.05 kg (50 g); WS20VB 0.2 kg.\n\nHmm, actually, is conversion safe? 50 g = 0.05 kg ✓. 200 g = 0.2 kg ✓. 3750 g = 3.75 kg ✓. 10 g = 0.01 kg ✓. Fine.\n\nminimum_load (Min, min bound, kg): WS10CW 0.05 [\"5)\"], WS20CW 0.05 [\"5)\"], WS20VB 0.2 [\"5)\"], WS6H 1 [\"5)\"], WS10H 3 [\"5)\"], VK* 0.05 [\"5)\"], EM 0.01 [\"5)\"].\n\nmax_verification_scale_intervals_n: WS10CW 6000, WS20CW 6000, WS20VB 6000, WS6H 6000, WS10H 6000, VK* 6000, EM 7500 — all footnotes [\"1)\",\"2)\",\"4)\"]. Note first-line for WS10CW/WS20CW & VK* is \"6000/5000\" — I take 6000.\n\nHmm, for VK* (table 5) first line is \"≤ 6000/5000^1)2)4)\", second \"≤ 6000^4)\". So multi: 6000; other: 6000. OK.\n\nSecond attribute: \"max_verification_scale_intervals_n_reduced\" — hmm naming. In tables: line 2 values: WS10CW/WS20CW: 5000 [\"4)\"]; WS20VB: 3000 [\"4)\"]; WS6H: 6000 [\"4)\"]; WS10H: 6000 [\"3)\"]; VK*: 6000 [\"4)\"]; EM: 7500 [\"4)\"].\n\nWait table 4 (WS10H): \"≤ 2 • 3000^1)2)4)<br>≤ 6000^3)\" — line2 = 6000, footnote 3). Yes.\n\nName it \"max_verification_scale_intervals_n_single_range_single_interval\"? Too interpretive. Maybe \"max_verification_scale_intervals_n_alternative_limit\"? I'll use \"max_verification_scale_intervals_n_single_interval\" — no. Let me just call it \"max_verification_scale_intervals_n_line2\"? Ugly. Hmm.\n\nActually, rethink footnotes: 1) applies to multiple range instruments; 2) applies to multi-interval instruments. These footnotes attach to the \"2 •\" (or to first line). So first line \"≤ 2 • 3000\" = for multiple-range/multi-interval instruments, n ≤ 6000 total. Second line \"≤ 3000\" = otherwise (single-range, single-interval) n ≤ 3000. For table 1: first line \"≤ 6000 / 5000\" — maybe multi-interval 6000 / multiple-range 5000?? and second line 5000 for single. Plausible.\n\nI'll name attributes:\n- \"max_verification_scale_intervals_n_multi_range_multi_interval\": value 6000 etc.\n- \"max_verification_scale_intervals_n_single_range_single_interval\": value 3000 etc.\n\nHmm but table 1's first line lacks \"2 •\". And second lines differ (5000 vs 3000). The label \"single_range_single_interval\" for line 2 is interpretive but reasonable given footnotes 1)&2) define the first line's scope. Eh — risk. Alternatively safer: keep line1/line2 in matrix_tables only and give ONE model_level attribute \"max_verification_scale_intervals_n\" with the primary (largest) number. Given the instruction \"Don't fabricate\", I'll go with the safer single attribute + full verbatim in matrix_tables. Decided: single model_level attr \"max_verification_scale_intervals_n\" with the line-1 leading number and footnotes [\"1)\",\"2)\",\"4)\"]; line 2 preserved in matrix_tables only.\n\nHmm, but wait: for table 1 and table 5 the line-1 is \"6000/5000\" (a slash pair). Leading number 6000. OK.\n\nmaximum_belt_speed (flat): WS10CW 1.47, WS20CW 1.47, WS6H 0.83 (m/s, max).\n\naccuracy_category per model: WS10CW: \"XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2)\" [\"3)\"]; all others: \"XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2)\" [\"4)\"]. Should I include? It's identical text with different footnotes. Include as model_level with footnote markers. Value string \"XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2)\". OK, include — footnotes differ meaningfully (3) vs 4)). I'll include.\n\ntemperature_range: same 0..40 °C for all → type_level. ✓.\n\nNow matrix_tables — consolidated:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"Technical data of the weighing modules (Tab. 1 to Tab. 6)\",\n  \"columns\": [\"module_type\", \"accuracy_category\", \"max_belt_speed\", \"max_n\", \"n_alternative\", \"e\", \"max_load\", \"min_load\", \"temperature_range\"],\n  \"column_units\": ... hmm units vary per row. Skip column_units or null.\n  \"rows\": [\n    {\"module_type\": \"WS10CW, WS20CW\", \"accuracy_category\": \"XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2) (3)\", \"max_belt_speed\": \"≤ 1.47 m/s\", ...}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nShould rows keep verbatim strings (with commas as decimal separators)? The schema says preserve tables in matrix_tables; strict structured values apply to \"characteristic value\" objects. For matrix_tables rows, I'll keep values mostly verbatim but normalize decimals to points and remove footnote markers into a footnotes field? Rule 2's structured-values requirement is for characteristics; matrix tables example used plain numbers (\"qmax\": 4.0). For these attribute-style cells, strings are acceptable. I'll use strings with decimal points (1,47 → 1.47) and put footnote markers as superscript refs like \"(3)\"? Rule 1 says never include footnote markers in labels; for matrix rows, markers can go in the \"footnotes\" array of the table. I'll keep cell text clean and add footnotes list at table level referencing footnote numbers, e.g. rows note footnotes in separate fields? Simpler: keep cells as clean strings without markers; table-level footnotes lists the applicable footnote definitions; and model_level/footnotes capture markers on structured values. Hmm, but per-row footnote differences (e.g., category footnote 3) vs 4)) get lost. I could add a \"footnote_markers\" array per row. The schema's rows are objects per row — extra keys allowed? The schema shows rows as objects; I'll add \"footnote_markers\" where applicable.\n\nLet me draft rows:\n\nRow 1: module_type \"WS10CW and WS20CW (Tab. 1)\", accuracy_category \"XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2)\", category_footnotes [\"3)\"], max_belt_speed \"≤ 1.47 m/s\", max_n \"≤ 6000/5000\", max_n_footnotes [\"1)\",\"2)\",\"4)\"], n_alternative \"≤ 5000\", n_alt_footnotes [\"4)\"], e \"≥ 1 g\", max_load \"≤ 15 kg\", min_load \"≥ 50 g\", min_load_footnotes [\"5)\"], temperature_range \"0 °C / +40 °C\".\n\nHmm, this is getting verbose. Alternative: 6 separate matrix_tables, one per Tab., each with rows as attribute-value pairs. That's verbose too.\n\nActually, maybe simpler: since each Tab is an attribute→value table, represent each as a matrix_table with columns [\"attribute\",\"value\"] and rows per attribute, preserving verbatim strings including footnote refs? Rule 1 prohibits footnote markers in labels — labels, not necessarily values. Values with footnote markers... rule 2 says footnote markers recorded in footnote_markers field of structured values (for characteristics). For matrix tables, I'll strip markers into per-row \"footnote_markers\".\n\nLet me just do the consolidated single matrix table with models as rows — cleanest. Columns:\n[\"module_type\", \"accuracy_category\", \"maximum_belt_speed\", \"max_verification_scale_intervals_n\", \"max_verification_scale_intervals_n_alt\", \"verification_scale_interval_e\", \"maximum_load\", \"minimum_load\", \"temperature_range\"]\n\nRows (10 module types; group WS10CW & WS20CW? They share Tab. 1 — I'll keep them as separate rows with identical data, or one row \"WS10CW, WS20CW\". One row is fine and faithful to \"modules of type WS10CW and WS20CW\". Similarly VK group: \"WS10VK1, WS10VK2, WS10VK5 and WS20VK\" one row. EM ... one row.)\n\nRows:\n1. WS10CW, WS20CW:\n   - accuracy_category: \"XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2)\" fm [\"3)\"]\n   - maximum_belt_speed: \"≤ 1.47 m/s\"\n   - n: \"≤ 6000 / 5000\" fm [\"1)\",\"2)\",\"4)\"]\n   - n_alt: \"≤ 5000\" fm [\"4)\"]\n   - e: \"≥ 1 g\"\n   - max_load: \"≤ 15 kg\"\n   - min_load: \"≥ 50 g\" fm [\"5)\"]\n   - temp: \"0 °C / +40 °C\"\n2. WS20VB:\n   - cat fm [\"4)\"]\n   - belt: \"≤ 1.25 m/s (0 < L ≤ 1 kg); ≤ 1.15 m/s (1 < L ≤ 6 kg); ≤ 0.79 m/s (6 kg < L ≤ 10 kg); ≤ 0.93 m/s (10 kg < L ≤ 15 kg)\"\n   - n: \"≤ 2 • 3000\" fm [\"1)\",\"2)\",\"4)\"]\n   - n_alt: \"≤ 3000\" fm [\"4)\"]\n   - e: \"≥ 2 g\"\n   - max: \"≤ 15 kg\"\n   - min: \"≥ 200 g\" fm [\"5)\"]\n   - temp: \"0 °C / +40 °C\"\n3. WS6H:\n   - cat fm [\"4)\"]\n   - belt: \"≤ 0.83 m/s\"\n   - n: \"≤ 2 • 3000\" fm [\"1)\",\"2)\",\"4)\"]\n   - n_alt: \"≤ 6000\" fm [\"4)\"]\n   - e: \"≥ 5 g\"\n   - max: \"≤ 30 kg\"\n   - min: \"≥ 1 kg\" fm [\"5)\"]\n4. WS10H:\n   - cat fm [\"4)\"]\n   - belt: \"≤ 0.67 m/s (0 < L < 3 kg); ≤ 1.00 m/s (3 kg ≤ L ≤ 60 kg)\"\n   - n: \"≤ 2 • 3000\" fm [\"1)\",\"2)\",\"4)\"]\n   - n_alt: \"≤ 6000\" fm [\"3)\"]\n   - e: \"≥ 10 g\"\n   - max: \"≤ 60 kg\"\n   - min: \"≥ 3 kg\" fm [\"5)\"]\n5. WS10VK1, WS10VK2, WS10VK5, WS20VK:\n   - cat fm [\"4)\"]\n   - belt: \"≤ 1.47 m/s (0 < L < 0.05 kg); ≤ 0.25 m/s (0.05 < L < 0.5 kg); ≤ 0.92 m/s (0.5 < L < 2 kg); ≤ 0.67 m/s (2 < L < 6 kg); ≤ 0.50 m/s (6 kg ≤ L ≤ 15 kg)\"\n   - n: \"≤ 6000 / 5000\" fm [\"1)\",\"2)\",\"4)\"]\n   - n_alt: \"≤ 6000\" fm [\"4)\"]\n   - e: \"≥ 1 g\"\n   - max: \"≤ 15 kg\"\n   - min: \"≥ 50 g\" fm [\"5)\"]\n6. EM ...:\n   - cat fm [\"4)\"]\n   - belt: \"≤ 0.83 m/s (0 g < L ≤ 10 g); ≤ 0.67 m/s (10 g < L ≤ 200 g); ≤ 0.50 m/s (200 g < L ≤ 3750 g)\"\n   - n: \"≤ 2 • 3750\" fm [\"1)\",\"2)\",\"4)\"]\n   - n_alt: \"≤ 7500\" fm [\"4)\"]\n   - e: \"≥ 0.1 g\"\n   - max: \"≤ 3750 g\"\n   - min: \"≥ 10 g\" fm [\"5)\"]\n\nfootnotes at table level: [\"1)\",\"2)\",\"3)\",\"4)\",\"5)\"] definitions or reference to global footnotes. I'll include short refs.\n\nHmm — putting \"≤ 2 • 3000\" — should I convert decimal commas? \"2 • 3000\" no comma. Belt \"1,47\"→\"1.47\" done. e \"≥ 0,1 g\"→\"≥ 0.1 g\" done.\n\nPer-row footnote arrays: I'll encode as \"footnote_markers\" object keys inside row? Rows are flat objects; I'll add keys like \"accuracy_category_footnotes\": [\"3)\"]? That clutters. Alternative: skip per-row markers in matrix table since structured characteristics already carry them. But category footnote differs (3) vs 4)) and n_alt footnote (3) vs 4)) — I'll include compact fields: \"footnotes\" per row as a string like \"category: 3); n: 1),2),4); n_alt: 4); min: 5)\". Hmm messy. \n\nCompromise: add suffix \"_fn\" keys. E.g.:\n{\"module_type\": \"WS10CW, WS20CW\", \"accuracy_category\": \"XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2)\", \"accuracy_category_fn\": \"3)\", \"maximum_belt_speed\": \"≤ 1.47 m/s\", \"n\": \"≤ 6000/5000\", \"n_fn\": \"1),2),4)\", \"n_alternative\": \"≤ 5000\", \"n_alternative_fn\": \"4)\", \"e\": \"≥ 1 g\", \"maximum_load\": \"≤ 15 kg\", \"minimum_load\": \"≥ 50 g\", \"minimum_load_fn\": \"5)\", \"temperature_range\": \"0 °C / +40 °C\"}\n\nI think that's acceptable for a preservation table. But hmm, the global footnotes[] already map markers→definitions; the model_level carries markers for the clean numeric attrs. To keep the JSON manageable, maybe drop per-cell fn in matrix rows EXCEPT where semantically important (category fn 3 vs 4; n_alt fn 3 vs 4). Ugh. I'll include the _fn keys — faithfulness wins.\n\nActually wait. Let me reconsider: is including both consolidated matrix table AND full model_level/config_level too redundant? The R137 example did exactly that (matrix_tables + model_level + model_family). So yes, include all three.\n\n**Common specifications vs type_level**: The common specs list is type_level. Note \"Maximum belt speed: ≤ 1,47 m/s\" and \"Maximum capacity: ≤ 60 kg\" — overall bounds. Good.\n\n**Number of the report roles**: \"the associated Report No. 1.12-4025390 (19 pages)\" — role \"type_evaluation_report\". Associated Test Reports /1../6 — role \"test_report\".\n\n**date_issued**: 2007-11-12 (signature date). ✓\n\nNow, model entries attributes: schema model: {model_id, label, attributes{}}. I could leave attributes {} and rely on model_level. I'll include minimal: model_id and maybe attributes: {} for all, with label only where descriptive. Keep attributes empty to avoid duplication.\n\nAlso should EM ... be in models? It's a series (\"EM ...\"). Include as model_id \"EM ...\" with label \"electromagnetic force compensation load cell module series\". Hmm, \"Don't fabricate\" — the description says \"with electromagnetic force compensation load cell (only weighing module series EM...)\". So label is justified.\n\nmodule labels: The tables call them \"weighing modules\". WS6H/WS10H: with external lever work. I'll add labels: WS6H \"weighing module with external lever work\", WS10H same; EM \"weighing module series with electromagnetic force compensation load cell\"; others \"weighing module\". Fine, or omit labels. I'll include modest labels.\n\n**type_level full list:**\n- mode_of_operation: \"Start-Stop operation or operation in motion\"\n- maximum_belt_speed: {max 1.47} m/s\n- maximum_capacity: {max 60} kg\n- verification_scale_interval: {min 0.1} g\n- max_verification_scale_intervals_n: {max 7500}\n- power_supply_voltage: 230 V\n- power_supply_frequency: {min 50, max 60} Hz\n- power_supply_type: \"AC\"\n- accuracy_category: \"XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2)\"\n- number_of_ranges_intervals: 2, fm [\"2)\",\"3)\"]\n- ratio_between_verification_scale_intervals: {max 3}, fm [\"1)\",\"2)\"]\n- temperature_range: {min 0, max 40} °C\n\nFor ratio: value {min: null, max: 3} — the expression is e_{i+1}/e_i < 3. Unit null. Attribute name conveys ratio. OK.\n\n**model_level list:**\n\n1. accuracy_category (string): values per model with fm.\nActually is accuracy_category varying? Only footnote differs. Include.\n- WS10CW: \"XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2)\" fm [\"3)\"]\n- WS20CW: same fm [\"3)\"]\n- WS20VB: fm [\"4)\"]\n- WS6H: fm [\"4)\"]\n- WS10H: fm [\"4)\"]\n- WS10VK1/2/5, WS20VK: fm [\"4)\"]\n- EM: fm [\"4)\"]\n\n2. maximum_belt_speed: WS10CW 1.47, WS20CW 1.47, WS6H 0.83 (m/s, {min:null,max:X}). Load-dependent ones in config_level. Also mention? For WS20VB, WS10H, VK*, EM the model_level would be... the overall max (1.25, 1.00, 1.47, 0.83)? To avoid duplication/confusion, only flat ones in model_level; document covers rest via config_level + matrix table. OK.\n\n3. verification_scale_interval: all models, unit g, value {min: X, max: null}.\n\n4. maximum_load: all models, unit kg, {min: null, max: X}. EM: 3.75 (from 3750 g). Hmm — conversion. Alternatively unit \"g\" for all: 15000, 15000, 15000, 30000, 60000, 15000×4, 3750. Then no conversion but changes source units for others. Or per-value unit override — schema doesn't support per-value units in model_level (unit at attribute level). I'll go with kg & 3.75 for EM and keep verbatim in matrix table. Similarly min_load: WS10CW 0.05, WS20CW 0.05, WS20VB 0.2, WS6H 1, WS10H 3, VK* 0.05, EM 0.01 (kg), fm [\"5)\"] each.\n\n5. min_load with footnote 5.\n\n6. max_verification_scale_intervals_n: WS10CW 6000, WS20CW 6000, WS20VB 6000, WS6H 6000, WS10H 6000, VK* 6000, EM 7500. fm [\"1)\",\"2)\",\"4)\"].\n\nWait — for WS10CW line1 \"6000/5000\": take 6000. OK.\n\nFootnote markers format: source shows \"1)\" style? In markdown: \"^{1) 2)}\" appears as <sup>1) 2)</sup>? Actually markdown shows \"2<sup>2) 3)</sup>\" and \"e<sub>i+1</sub>/e<sub>i</sub> < 3 <sup>1) 2)</sup>\" and \"≥ 50 g<sup>5)</sup>\" and \"≤ 6000 / 5000<sup>1) 2) 4)</sup>\". So markers are \"1)\", \"2)\", etc. Schema example: [\"1)\"]. Good.\n\nAlso the n superscripts in tables: \"≤ 6000 / 5000<sup>1) 2) 4)</sup>\" → fm [\"1)\",\"2)\",\"4)\"].\n\n**components?** Should the load cell (electromagnetic force compensation or strain gauge) be a component? The cert describes equipment options but doesn't name specific load cell types. Omit components (no named sub-assembly types). Hmm, \"weighing module\" WS... are sub-assemblies of the checkweigher CWM... Actually the certified type CWM is the checkweigher comprising weighing modules WS*/EM*. Could record modules as components? But they're the models of the family themselves. I think model_family.models = the weighing modules is the right mapping (\"Technical data of the weighing modules of type WS10CW...\"). Skip components.\n\n**certificate.scheme**: omit.\n\nLet me also double check \"Category: XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2)\" — the R51 accuracy classes are actually XIII and XIIIH... yes R51-1:2006 uses accuracy classes XIII (automatic gravimetric filling? no...). Actually R51 Automatic catchweighing instruments: classes XIII, XIIIH, Y... I'll just record as-is.\n\n**description** for certified_type: combine page 2 \"Identification of the pattern (continued)\" text.\n\n**issuing_authority.person_title**: \"Direktor und Professor\" — from signature block for Dr. P. Zervos. OK.\n\nNow assemble final JSON. Also \"R3-0033\" — form number, ignore.\n\nrecommendation: {\"id\": \"R51-1\", \"edition\": 2006, \"accuracy_classes\": [\"XIII(1)\", \"XIIIH(x ≥ 2)\"]}. scheme: omit.\n\ntest_reports:\n[\n {\"id\": \"1.12-4025390\", \"pages\": 19, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"},\n {\"id\": \"1.12-4025390/1\", \"pages\": 66, \"role\": \"test_report\"},\n {\"id\": \"1.12-4025390/2\", \"pages\": 35, \"role\": \"test_report\"},\n {\"id\": \"1.12-4025390/3\", \"pages\": 47, \"role\": \"test_report\"},\n {\"id\": \"1.12-4025390/4\", \"pages\": 60, \"role\": \"test_report\"},\n {\"id\": \"1.12-4025390/5\", \"pages\": 21, \"role\": \"test_report\"},\n {\"id\": \"1.12-4025390/6\", \"pages\": 63, \"role\": \"test_report\"}\n]\n\nDates for reports: none given.\n\nfootnotes array as above.\n\nCertificate: {\"number\": \"R51/2006-DE1-07.05\", \"page_total\": 4, \"member_state\": \"Germany\", \"date_issued\": \"2007-11-12\"}. \n\nNow write it out. Ensure JSON parseable, no LaTeX. Avoid \"≥\" etc.? Unicode symbols fine in strings (unit \"°C\", \"m/s\", \"kg\", \"g\", \"V\", \"Hz\"). For conditions, \"0 < L ≤ 1 kg\" fine.\n\nOne more check — decimal conversions: 1,47→1.47; 1,25→1.25; 1,15→1.15; 0,79→0.79; 0,93→0.93; 0,83→0.83; 0,67→0.67; 1,00→1.0; 0,25→0.25; 0,92→0.92; 0,50→0.5; 0,05→0.05; 0,1→0.1. ✓\n\nconfig_level conditions: include model prefix. I'll format: \"WS20VB: 0 kg < L ≤ 1 kg\"? Source: \"≤ 1,25 m/s (0 < L ≤ 1 kg)\". Condition: \"WS20VB, 0 < L ≤ 1 kg\". Good.\n\nLet me write model_level entries carefully.\n\nmodel_level:\n\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"accuracy_category\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"model\": \"WS10CW\", \"value\": \"XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2)\", \"footnote_markers\": [\"3)\"]},\n      {\"model\": \"WS20CW\", \"value\": \"XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2)\", \"footnote_markers\": [\"3)\"]},\n      {\"model\": \"WS20VB\", \"value\": \"XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2)\", \"footnote_markers\": [\"4)\"]},\n      {\"model\": \"WS6H\", \"value\": \"XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2)\", \"footnote_markers\": [\"4)\"]},\n      {\"model\": \"WS10H\", \"value\": \"XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2)\", \"footnote_markers\": [\"4)\"]},\n      {\"model\": \"WS10VK1\", \"value\": \"XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2)\", \"footnote_markers\": [\"4)\"]},\n      {\"model\": \"WS10VK2\", \"value\": \"XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2)\", \"footnote_markers\": [\"4)\"]},\n      {\"model\": \"WS10VK5\", \"value\": \"XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2)\", \"footnote_markers\": [\"4)\"]},\n      {\"model\": \"WS20VK\", \"value\": \"XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2)\", \"footnote_markers\": [\"4)\"]},\n      {\"model\": \"EM ...\", \"value\": \"XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2)\", \"footnote_markers\": [\"4)\"]}\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"maximum_belt_speed\",\n    \"unit_symbol\": \"m/s\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"model\": \"WS10CW\", \"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 1.47}},\n      {\"model\": \"WS20CW\", \"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 1.47}},\n      {\"model\": \"WS6H\", \"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 0.83}}\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"max_verification_scale_intervals_n\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"model\": \"WS10CW\", \"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 6000}, \"footnote_markers\": [\"1)\",\"2)\",\"4)\"]},\n      ... etc\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"verification_scale_interval_e\",\n    \"unit_symbol\": \"g\",\n    \"values\": [... {min: X, max: null}]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"maximum_load\",\n    \"unit_symbol\": \"kg\",\n    \"values\": [...]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"minimum_load\",\n    \"unit_symbol\": \"kg\",\n    \"values\": [... with fm 5)]\n  }\n]\n```\n\nAttribute naming: \"max_verification_scale_intervals_n\" → canonical maybe \"max_number_of_verification_scale_intervals\" or per R60 style \"max_load_cell_intervals_nlc\" suggests keeping \"n\". I'll use \"max_verification_scale_intervals_n\". And e: \"verification_scale_interval_e\". Max: \"maximum_load_max\" per table label \"Maximum load Max\" → \"maximum_load_max\"? Rule examples: \"Maximum capacity (Emax)\" → maximum_capacity. So \"Maximum load Max\" → \"maximum_load_max\"? Or \"maximum_load\". I'll use \"maximum_load_max\" and \"minimum_load_min\" to be faithful... hmm those read oddly. The examples show canonical simplification: \"Maximum capacity Emax\" → \"maximum_capacity\". So \"Maximum load Max\" → \"maximum_load\", \"Minimum load Min\" → \"minimum_load\", \"Verification scale interval e\" → \"verification_scale_interval\", \"Number n of verification scale intervals\" → \"number_of_verification_scale_intervals_n\" → simpler \"max_verification_scale_intervals_n\" (values are maxima). I'll go: \"verification_scale_interval\", \"maximum_load\", \"minimum_load\", \"max_verification_scale_intervals_n\", \"maximum_belt_speed\".\n\nFor type_level \"Verification scale interval e ≥ 0.1 g\" → \"verification_scale_interval\": {min: 0.1}. And \"Number n of verification scale intervals: n ≤ 7500\" → \"number_of_verification_scale_intervals\": {max: 7500}. Use \"number_of_verification_scale_intervals\" at type level and \"max_verification_scale_intervals_n\" at model level? Inconsistent; unify: \"number_of_verification_scale_intervals\" both places. OK.\n\nLet me finalize names:\n- mode_of_operation\n- maximum_belt_speed\n- maximum_capacity (type-level overall)\n- verification_scale_interval\n- number_of_verification_scale_intervals\n- power_supply_voltage\n- power_supply_type\n- power_supply_frequency\n- accuracy_category\n- number_of_ranges_intervals\n- ratio_between_verification_scale_intervals\n- temperature_range\n- maximum_load\n- minimum_load\n\nNow matrix table rows include verbatim strings. Compose \"n\" strings: \"≤ 6000 / 5000\" — keep as \"≤ 6000/5000\".\n\nAlright, also config_level:\n\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"maximum_belt_speed\",\n    \"axis\": \"load\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"WS20VB, 0 < L ≤ 1 kg\", \"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 1.25}, \"unit_symbol\": \"m/s\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"WS20VB, 1 < L ≤ 6 kg\", \"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 1.15}, \"unit_symbol\": \"m/s\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"WS20VB, 6 kg < L ≤ 10 kg\", \"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 0.79}, \"unit_symbol\": \"m/s\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"WS20VB, 10 kg < L ≤ 15 kg\", \"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 0.93}, \"unit_symbol\": \"m/s\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"WS10H, 0 < L < 3 kg\", \"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 0.67}, \"unit_symbol\": \"m/s\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"WS10H, 3 kg ≤ L ≤ 60 kg\", \"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 1.0}, \"unit_symbol\": \"m/s\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"WS10VK1, WS10VK2, WS10VK5, WS20VK, 0 < L < 0.05 kg\", \"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 1.47}, \"unit_symbol\": \"m/s\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"WS10VK1, WS10VK2, WS10VK5, WS20VK, 0.05 < L < 0.5 kg\", \"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 0.25}, \"unit_symbol\": \"m/s\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"WS10VK1, WS10VK2, WS10VK5, WS20VK, 0.5 < L < 2 kg\", \"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 0.92}, \"unit_symbol\": \"m/s\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"WS10VK1, WS10VK2, WS10VK5, WS20VK, 2 < L < 6 kg\", \"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 0.67}, \"unit_symbol\": \"m/s\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"WS10VK1, WS10VK2, WS10VK5, WS20VK, 6 kg ≤ L ≤ 15 kg\", \"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 0.5}, \"unit_symbol\": \"m/s\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"EM ..., 0 g < L ≤ 10 g\", \"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 0.83}, \"unit_symbol\": \"m/s\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"EM ..., 10 g < L ≤ 200 g\", \"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 0.67}, \"unit_symbol\": \"m/s\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"EM ..., 200 g < L ≤ 3750 g\", \"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 0.5}, \"unit_symbol\": \"m/s\"}\n    ]\n  }\n]\n```\n\nNow matrix_tables. Single consolidated table:\n\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Technical data of the weighing modules per type (Tab. 1 to Tab. 6)\",\n    \"columns\": [\"module_type\", \"accuracy_category\", \"maximum_belt_speed\", \"number_of_verification_scale_intervals\", \"number_of_verification_scale_intervals_alternative\", \"verification_scale_interval\", \"maximum_load\", \"minimum_load\", \"temperature_range\"],\n    \"rows\": [ ... ],\n    \"footnotes\": [\"1) This applies to multiple range instruments.\", \"2) This applies to multi-interval instruments.\", \"3) ...\", \"4) ...\", \"5) ...\"]\n  }\n]\n```\n\nRows with _fn keys:\n\nRow1:\n{\"module_type\": \"WS10CW, WS20CW\", \"accuracy_category\": \"XIII(1); XIIIH(x ≥ 2)\", \"accuracy_category_fn\": \"3)\", \"maximum_belt_speed\": \"≤ 1.47 m/s\", \"number_of_verification_scale_intervals\": \"≤ 6000/5000\", \"n_fn\": \"1),2),4)\", \"number_of_verification_scale_intervals_alternative\": \"≤ 5000\", \"n_alt_fn\": \"4)\", \"verification_scale_interval\": \"≥ 1 g\", \"maximum_load\": \"≤ 15 kg\", \"minimum_load\": \"≥ 50 g\", \"minimum_load_fn\": \"5)\", \"temperature_range\": \"0 °C / +40 °C\"}\n\nHmm \"_fn\" keys inconsistent naming (\"n_fn\" vs full). Use full: \"number_of_verification_scale_intervals_fn\": \"1), 2), 4)\". OK let me use \"<col>_fn\" consistently.\n\nRow2 WS20VB: cat_fn \"4)\", belt \"≤ 1.25 m/s (0 < L ≤ 1 kg); ≤ 1.15 m/s (1 < L ≤ 6 kg); ≤ 0.79 m/s (6 kg < L ≤ 10 kg); ≤ 0.93 m/s (10 kg < L ≤ 15 kg)\", n \"≤ 2 • 3000\" fn \"1), 2), 4)\", n_alt \"≤ 3000\" fn \"4)\", e \"≥ 2 g\", max \"≤ 15 kg\", min \"≥ 200 g\" fn \"5)\", temp \"0 °C / +40 °C\".\n\nRow3 WS6H: belt \"≤ 0.83 m/s\", n \"≤ 2 • 3000\", n_alt \"≤ 6000\" fn \"4)\", e \"≥ 5 g\", max \"≤ 30 kg\", min \"≥ 1 kg\" fn \"5)\".\n\nRow4 WS10H: belt \"≤ 0.67 m/s (0 < L < 3 kg); ≤ 1.00 m/s (3 kg ≤ L ≤ 60 kg)\", n \"≤ 2 • 3000\", n_alt \"≤ 6000\" fn \"3)\", e \"≥ 10 g\", max \"≤ 60 kg\", min \"≥ 3 kg\" fn \"5)\".\n\nRow5 VK group: belt \"≤ 1.47 m/s (0 < L < 0.05 kg); ≤ 0.25 m/s (0.05 < L < 0.5 kg); ≤ 0.92 m/s (0.5 < L < 2 kg); ≤ 0.67 m/s (2 < L < 6 kg); ≤ 0.50 m/s (6 kg ≤ L ≤ 15 kg)\", n \"≤ 6000/5000\", n_alt \"≤ 6000\" fn \"4)\", e \"≥ 1 g\", max \"≤ 15 kg\", min \"≥ 50 g\" fn \"5)\".\n\nRow6 EM ...: belt \"≤ 0.83 m/s (0 g < L ≤ 10 g); ≤ 0.67 m/s (10 g < L ≤ 200 g); ≤ 0.50 m/s (200 g < L ≤ 3750 g)\", n \"≤ 2 • 3750\", n_alt \"≤ 7500\" fn \"4)\", e \"≥ 0.1 g\", max \"≤ 3750 g\", min \"≥ 10 g\" fn \"5)\".\n\nAlso give the matrix a \"description\" field: \"Consolidated from Tab. 1 to Tab. 6; captions appear below each table. Tab. 1: WS10CW and WS20CW; Tab. 2: WS20VB; Tab. 3: WS6H; Tab. 4: WS10H; Tab. 5: WS10VK1, WS10VK2, WS10VK5 and WS20VK; Tab. 6: EM ...\" — good for traceability.\n\nNow footnote texts (extract exactly, cleaned):\n1) \"This applies to multiple range instruments.\"\n2) \"This applies to multi-interval instruments.\"\n3) \"This specification applies only to weighing instruments with parameters being generally valid over the complete weighing range for all products and weighing in motion (dynamic weighing). For single products having especially their own Price Look Up and/or their own product-specific parameters greater tare loads are allowed if they are approved by the LMC.\"\n4) \"For weighing instruments of the categories XIIIH(x ≥ 2) and Y(b) the number of verification scale intervals is limited to ni ≤ 1000.\" — has n_i subscript; write \"ni ≤ 1000\" (avoid LaTeX; \"n_i\"? plain \"ni\" or \"n(i)\". I'll write \"n_i ≤ 1000\"? Underscore ok, not LaTeX. Hmm, canonical says no LaTeX; plain text subscript fine as \"ni\". I'll use \"ni ≤ 1000\".) Actually \"n<sub>i</sub> ≤ 1000\" in HTML → \"ni ≤ 1000\". Fine.\n5) \"The same minimum capacities as for category Y according to MID, Annex I, chap. II No 5, may be applied to category X. Greater minimum loads may also result from the LMC in general.\"\n\ncertified_type.category: \"Checkweigher\". type_designations: [\"CWM\", \"WS10CW\", \"WS20CW\", \"WS20VB\", \"WS6H\", \"WS10H\", \"WS10VK1\", \"WS10VK2\", \"WS10VK5\", \"WS20VK\", \"EM\"]. Should I keep \"...\"? \"Type: CWM ...\" — the ellipsis indicates variants/extensions. I'll record \"CWM ...\" and \"EM ...\" verbatim? Type designations ideally are identifiers; \"CWM ...\" as written. Hmm. I'll use \"CWM\" and \"EM\" without ellipsis but description notes series. Actually keeping \"EM ...\" communicates \"series with various suffixes\". I'll keep ellipsis: \"CWM ...\", \"EM ...\". Slight risk either way; verbatim wins.\n\nmodel_family.models: include label + attributes {}:\n- WS10CW: label \"Weighing module WS10CW (Tab. 1)\"... keep simple: label null? I'll include short labels:\n  - WS10CW: \"weighing module of type WS10CW\" — redundant. I'll set attributes {} and label only for EM: \"series with electromagnetic force compensation load cell\" and WS6H/WS10H: \"with external lever work\". Others label null/omit.\n\nfamily_name: \"CWM\". Hmm — is \"CWM\" a family name? \"Checkweigher Type: CWM ...\" — the type is CWM series. The modules are sub-variants. I'd say family_name \"CWM\". OK.\n\nFinal check on certificate.scheme — omit. project_number — omit.\n\ndate_issued: \"2007-11-12\". \n\nOne consideration: \"Page 1 of 4 pages\" → page_total 4. ✓\n\nAlso include issuing_authority.oiml_issuer_id? Not present. Omit.\n\nLet me also double check power supply: \"230 V AC, 50/60 Hz\" → voltage 230 V, type AC, frequency 50–60 Hz. ✓\n\nWrite final JSON now. Keep it valid: no trailing commas, proper nulls.\n\nAlso description for certified_type — assemble:\n\n\"Automatic electromechanical weighing instrument designed as automatic catchweighing instrument, checkweigher, weigh labeller or weigh-price labeller, equipped with external lever work (only weighing module series WS6H and WS10H) or without external lever work, and with electromagnetic force compensation load cell (only weighing module series EM ...) or with strain gauge load cells; performed as single or multi interval instrument or single or multiple range instrument.\"\n\nGood.\n\nNumber formatting: use 1.0 or 1? JSON: 1.0 fine; I'll write 1.0 as 1 for \"≤ 1,00\"? Keep 1.0 → 1. Eh, 1 is fine.\n\nLet me write the complete JSON.",
